---
import Guide from '../../layouts/Guide.astro';
import Analogy from '../../components/Analogy.astro';
import { DEFAULT_PART as D } from '../../lib/design.ts';
---

<Guide slug="design-for-printing">
  <h2>Why it matters</h2>
  <p>
    Most failed prints fail in the design, long before the printer warms up. A shape that looks fine on screen can be
    impossible to build from the bottom up. Four checks catch nearly all of it, and they cost nothing to fix while the
    part is still a file.
  </p>

  <h2>The intuition</h2>
  <Analogy title="Building with bricks, with no scaffolding">
    <p>
      A printer stacks layers the way a bricklayer stacks bricks. Each new brick can stick out a little past the one
      below, but only a little; lean out too far and it falls. And you can't lay a brick in mid-air, though you can
      lay a short lintel across a gap. That's the whole game: <strong>every bit of plastic needs something under it</strong>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Where it breaks down: molten plastic is stickier than a brick, so a printer can cheat a bit. It can lean out to
      about 45° and span small gaps in mid-air, which a bricklayer can't.
    </p>
  </Analogy>

  <h2 id="try-it">Try it</h2>
  <p>Set your part's four trickiest features and see what the printer will make of them.</p>
  <div class="wide">
    <div class="calc" data-design>
      <div class="calc-head"><span>Printability check</span><span>rules of thumb · 0.4 mm nozzle</span></div>
      <div class="calc-body">
        <form data-form onsubmit="return false">
          <div class="field">
            <label for="d-o">Steepest overhang <span class="range-val" data-v="overhangDeg"></span></label>
            <input id="d-o" name="overhangDeg" type="range" min="0" max="90" step="5" value={D.overhangDeg} />
          </div>
          <div class="field">
            <label for="d-b">Longest bridge <span class="range-val" data-v="bridgeMm"></span></label>
            <input id="d-b" name="bridgeMm" type="range" min="0" max="120" step="5" value={D.bridgeMm} />
          </div>
          <div class="field">
            <label for="d-w">Thinnest wall <span class="range-val" data-v="wallMm"></span></label>
            <input id="d-w" name="wallMm" type="range" min="0.4" max="3" step="0.1" value={D.wallMm} />
          </div>
          <div class="field">
            <label for="d-c">Gap between moving parts <span class="range-val" data-v="clearanceMm"></span></label>
            <input id="d-c" name="clearanceMm" type="range" min="0.05" max="1" step="0.05" value={D.clearanceMm} />
          </div>
          <p class="hint">Overhang is measured from vertical: 0° is a straight wall, 90° is a flat ceiling.</p>
        </form>
        <div class="calc-out" aria-live="polite">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 300 170" class="demo" role="img" aria-label="Side view of the overhang and bridge">
            <desc>A side view of a printed wall leaning out at the chosen overhang angle, and a bridge spanning two posts with sag shown when it is too long.</desc>
            <line x1="10" y1="150" x2="290" y2="150" stroke="var(--line-2)"></line>
            <path data-over fill="var(--hot)" opacity="0.9"></path>
            <path data-over-sup fill="none" stroke="var(--uv-2)" stroke-dasharray="3 3"></path>
            <rect x="170" y="90" width="12" height="60" fill="var(--hot)"></rect>
            <rect data-post x="260" y="90" width="12" height="60" fill="var(--hot)"></rect>
            <path data-bridge fill="none" stroke="var(--hot)" stroke-width="7" stroke-linecap="round"></path>
            <text x="70" y="166" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl">overhang</text>
            <text x="222" y="166" text-anchor="middle" class="lbl">bridge</text>
          </svg>
          <div class="overall" data-overall></div>
          <ul class="checks" data-checks></ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>Worked example: a phone stand</h2>
  <p>The default settings above are a simple phone stand. Each feature against its rule:</p>
  <div class="math">{`Overhang    45° back rest           ≤ 45°        prints cleanly
Bridge      20 mm cable slot        ≤ 30 mm      spans without sagging
Wall        1.2 mm                  ≥ 1.2 mm     three nozzle passes (3 × 0.4)
Clearance   0.30 mm hinge gap       0.25–0.5     moves freely

All four pass, so the part needs no supports.`}</div>
  <p>
    Push the overhang to 80° and the verdict flips: it now needs supports, which cost time, plastic and a rough
    surface where they touch. Often the fix is free: turn the part over, or split it in two flat-bottomed halves.
  </p>

  <h2>Where this stops</h2>
  <div class="stops">
    <ul>
      <li><strong>They're rules of thumb.</strong> A well-tuned printer with good cooling beats them; a fast draft print falls short of them.</li>
      <li><strong>Materials differ.</strong> PLA bridges and overhangs best. PETG and TPU sag sooner.</li>
      <li><strong>Orientation changes everything.</strong> The same part can be easy one way up and impossible another. Layers are also the weak direction: a hook printed lying down is far stronger than one printed standing up.</li>
      <li><strong>Not sure?</strong> Send the file. I'll check it and suggest changes before anything is printed.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <h2>References</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Prusa Knowledge Base: "Modeling with 3D printing in mind".</li>
    <li>Redwood, Schöffer &amp; Garret, <em>The 3D Printing Handbook</em> (3D Hubs, 2017), FDM design rules.</li>
  </ul>
</Guide>

<script>
  import { checkPart, overall, type PartInput } from '../../lib/design.ts';
  const root = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-design]');
  if (root) {
    const form = root.querySelector<HTMLFormElement>('[data-form]')!;
    const $ = (s: string) => root.querySelector<SVGElement | HTMLElement>(s)!;
    const UNIT: Record<string, (n: number) => string> = {
      overhangDeg: (n) => `${n}°`,
      bridgeMm: (n) => `${n} mm`,
      wallMm: (n) => `${n.toFixed(1)} mm`,
      clearanceMm: (n) => `${n.toFixed(2)} mm`,
    };
    const WORD = { good: 'Prints cleanly', care: 'Printable, with care', problem: 'Needs a rethink' };
    const render = () => {
      const f = new FormData(form);
      const p = Object.fromEntries([...f.entries()].map(([k, v]) => [k, Number(v)])) as unknown as PartInput;
      for (const el of root.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-v]')) el.textContent = UNIT[el.dataset.v!](p[el.dataset.v as keyof PartInput]);
      const checks = checkPart(p);
      const all = overall(checks);
      $('[data-overall]').className = `overall ${all}`;
      $('[data-overall]').textContent = WORD[all];
      $('[data-checks]').innerHTML = checks.map((c) => `<li class="${c.verdict}"><i></i><span><b>${c.title}</b>${c.note}</span></li>`).join('');
      // overhang: a 14-wide wall leaning out from (40,150), 80 tall
      const a = (p.overhangDeg * Math.PI) / 180;
      const len = 80;
      const dx = Math.min(100, Math.sin(a) * len), dy = Math.max(6, Math.cos(a) * len);
      $('[data-over]').setAttribute('d', `M30 150 L44 150 L${44 + dx} ${150 - dy} L${30 + dx} ${150 - dy} Z`);
      $('[data-over-sup]').setAttribute('d', p.overhangDeg > 60 ? `M${30 + dx * 0.5} 150 V${150 - dy * 0.5} M${30 + dx} 150 V${150 - dy}` : '');
      // bridge: span scales with length, sag when long
      const span = 20 + (p.bridgeMm / 120) * 100;
      const x2 = 176 + span;
      $('[data-post]').setAttribute('x', String(x2 - 6));
      const sag = p.bridgeMm <= 30 ? 0 : p.bridgeMm <= 60 ? 8 : 22;
      $('[data-bridge]').setAttribute('d', `M176 90 Q${(176 + x2) / 2} ${90 + sag * 2} ${x2} 90`);
    };
    form.addEventListener('input', render);
    render();
  }
</script>

<style>
  .demo {
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: 10px;
    background: var(--ink-2);
  }
  .lbl {
    font: 500 10px var(--mono);
    fill: var(--text-3);
  }
  .overall {
    font: 700 1.5rem var(--display);
    letter-spacing: -0.03em;
  }
  .overall:global(.good),
  .checks :global(.good b) {
    color: var(--mint);
  }
  .overall:global(.care),
  .checks :global(.care b) {
    color: var(--warn);
  }
  .overall:global(.problem),
  .checks :global(.problem b) {
    color: var(--danger);
  }
  .checks {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    display: grid;
    gap: 8px;
  }
  .checks :global(li) {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 10px 1fr;
    gap: 10px;
    align-items: start;
    font-size: 0.88rem;
    color: var(--text-2);
  }
  .checks :global(i) {
    width: 10px;
    height: 10px;
    margin-top: 6px;
    border-radius: 3px;
    background: currentColor;
  }
  .checks :global(.good) {
    color: var(--mint);
  }
  .checks :global(.care) {
    color: var(--warn);
  }
  .checks :global(.problem) {
    color: var(--danger);
  }
  .checks :global(span) {
    color: var(--text-2);
  }
  .checks :global(b) {
    display: block;
  }
</style>
